#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:69
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using &ktouch;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:72
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr "Introduccion"

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:75
#, no-c-format
msgid "What is &ktouch;?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:76
#, no-c-format
msgid "&ktouch; is a program for learning to touch type."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:77
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&ktouch; provides you with text to train on and adjusts to different levels "
"depending on how good you are. It shows your keyboard and indicates which key "
"to press next and the correct finger to use."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:82
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>The main screen of &ktouch;</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:88
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>The main screen of &ktouch;</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:93
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&ktouch; is an excellent touch typing tutor. You learn typing with all fingers, "
"step by step, without having to look down at the keyboard all the time to find "
"your keys (which slows you down a lot). It is convenient for all ages and the "
"perfect typing tutor for schools, universities and individuals. It is free and "
"licensed under the &GNU; Public License, so you will never have to pay anything "
"for the program or any update."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:104
#, no-c-format
msgid "Features"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In its current version, &ktouch; 1.5 has quite a number of helpful features, "
"such as:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:109
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Support for many different training lectures in many languages including "
"language specific text fonts and a comfortable lecture editor"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:112
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Support for different keyboard layouts with the ability to use user-defined "
"layouts"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:114
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"During your training session comprehensive statistical information is obtained "
"to help you (or your teacher) analyse your progress"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:118
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Many other features are included and will be discussed in the appropriate "
"chapters of this manual."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:128
#, no-c-format
msgid "Learning touch typing with &ktouch;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:130
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Here's another screenshot of &ktouch; in action, this time with a german "
"keyboard and training lecture:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:134
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Main screen of &ktouch;</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:140
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Main screen of &ktouch;</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:145
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Even though learning touch typing with &ktouch; and using the program should be "
"very simple we will look a bit closer at &ktouch; in the following sections."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:150
#, no-c-format
msgid "Getting started"
msgstr "Per començar"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:152
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you start &ktouch; you can see the screen shown above. The main program "
"window shows three main sections:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:156
#, no-c-format
msgid "Some typing statistics in info boxes at the top"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:158
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The text you need to type (in the teacher's line) and the text that you have "
"typed so far (in the student's line)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:160
#, no-c-format
msgid "The keyboard (that hopefully looks like yours)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:163
#, no-c-format
msgid "We look at these sections later again."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:164
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Ok, the first time you start &ktouch; it will open a lecture and select a "
"keyboard layout for the locale and keybord defined in the &kcontrolcenter;. To "
"adjust the keyboard layout go to <menuchoice><guimenu>Settings</guimenu>"
"<guisubmenu>Keyboard Layouts...</guisubmenu></menuchoice> "
"and select the country you're in (or some country which uses the same keyboard "
"layout as you). If your keyboard is not included, you can always define your "
"own keyboard (see <link linkend=\"create_keyboards\">"
"Creating your own keyboard</link>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:174
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Once you have your keyboard selected, choose your training lecture. The first "
"line in your current lecture and level is shown in the teacher's line. You can "
"select any of the default lectures via <menuchoice><guimenu>Training</guimenu>"
"<guisubmenu>Default Lectures</guisubmenu></menuchoice> "
"or open a user defined lecture file (&eg; if your teacher gives you a special "
"lecture to practice on) with <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>"
"Open Lecture...</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. You may even create your own "
"lecture (see <link linkend=\"create_lectures\">Creating your own lecture</link>"
"). When you have selected keyboard and lecture you can start typing."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:186
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Place your hand on the keyboard as indicated by the 8 marked finger keys "
"(thumbs over the space bar) and start typing the text shown in the teacher's "
"line. Each key you need to press is highlighted on the keyboard and if you need "
"to move a finger from its resting position the target key and the appropriate "
"finger key is highlighted. Try to move your hands as little as possible and "
"after you typed a character always try to return your fingers to their resting "
"positions."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:195
#, no-c-format
msgid "The key colors indicate which finger needs to press which key."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:197
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"While you type you may make mistakes and hit the wrong key. In that case the "
"student's line will change its color (per default to red) and you have to hit "
"Backspace to erase the wrong character (or characters) again."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:202
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you have successfully completed a line of text you need to press the "
"&Enter; key to advance to the next line."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:209
#, no-c-format
msgid "Lectures and levels"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:210
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"As already mentioned above you need to select a training lecture which contains "
"all the text you want to practice. Usually the difficulty of the text is "
"gradually increasing and so most lectures contain various difficulty levels. "
"However, some lectures may just contain a single level and text (&eg; a "
"newspaper article to test your skills)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:217
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In the info boxes on top of the &ktouch; main window you see the current level "
"on the left. The small buttons to the right allow you to manually switch "
"levels. Normally this happens automatically (more about this in the section "
"<link linkend=\"training_options\">Training Options</link>"
"). The right info box shows a brief information text about the level. Usually "
"it gives you a list of characters that are new in this level. The middle two "
"info boxes show your current typing speed (in characters per minute) and your "
"correctness. These values are Level Statistics and whenever you start a new "
"level these values are resetted. However, the program still remembers your "
"previous statistics (see section <link linkend=\"statistics\">"
"Statistics in &ktouch;</link>)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:232
#, no-c-format
msgid "Starting and stopping a training session"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:233
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"While you type the text of a training lecture you are in a practice session. "
"You can start a new practice session using <menuchoice><guimenu>"
"Training</guimenu><guimenuitem>Start New Session</guimenuitem></menuchoice> "
"or the appropriate button in the toolbar. Level changes (automatic or manual) "
"do not change your training session. You stay in this training session until "
"you start a new training session, select a new lecture (which automatically "
"starts a new training session) or quit the program."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:243
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you open any dialog in &ktouch; while you are in a training session the "
"training is paused and the timer is stopped until you hit the next key. You can "
"manually pause the session using <menuchoice><guimenu>Training</guimenu>"
"<guimenuitem>Pause Session</guimenuitem></menuchoice> "
"or the appropriate button in the toolbar."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:255
#, no-c-format
msgid "Statistics in &ktouch;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:256
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&ktouch; remembers you! Basically whatever you type is recorded and some useful "
"(and sometimes just interesting) statistics are created. &ktouch; stores "
"statistical data based on the lecture file you use for training. For every "
"lecture file a separate history is kept. &ktouch; also distinguishes between "
"level and practice session statistics. This is important to prevent cheating "
":-)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:265
#, no-c-format
msgid "Training Session Statistics"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:267
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Current Training Statistics</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:271
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Current Training Statistics</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:274
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Whenever you start a new training session a new statistics record is started. "
"&ktouch; remembers several things to assess your skill and to help you identify "
"problem keys:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:279
#, no-c-format
msgid "How many keys were pressed in total?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:280
#, no-c-format
msgid "How many were correctly pressed?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:281
#, no-c-format
msgid "Which keys where missed and how often?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:282
#, no-c-format
msgid "How many correct words did you type?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: simpara
#: index.docbook:283
#, no-c-format
msgid "How much time did you need?"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:285
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Using training session statistics you and &ktouch; can evaluate your overall "
"performance and skill in a certain training lecture."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:290
#, no-c-format
msgid "Level Statistics"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:291
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For each level in your training session (&ie; all levels you typed text in) the "
"same statistics as for the training session are obtained. Using level "
"statistics data you can investigate your progress in each level of a training "
"lecture (and you will notice how your skill increases level by level and you "
"can concentrate on the levels which still cause problems)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:300
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Statistics Dialog"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:301
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<menuchoice><guimenu>Training</guimenu><guimenuitem>"
"Lecture Statistics</guimenuitem></menuchoice> or the statistics button in the "
"toolbar opens the statistics dialog. It displays your current training and "
"level statistics and shows a graphical history of previous training sessions."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:306
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When you open the statistics dialog you can select a <guilabel>"
"Current Training Session</guilabel> statistics page and a <guilabel>"
"Current Level Statistics</guilabel> page. The first page gives you a summary "
"about everything you typed so far. The <guilabel>Current Level "
"Statistics</guilabel> page only contains the stats for the current level. This "
"is useful to distinguish between overall performance and skill in certain "
"levels."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:314
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The third statistics page gives you a graphical overview of your typing skill "
"by offering several choices. A note about the time scaling option. Whenever a "
"session or level statistic is obtained, also the date is stored. So you can "
"also see how your skill dropped in long training breaks and when you last "
"practiced a certain lecture or level."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:322
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Training Statistics Chart</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:326
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Training Statistics Chart</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:335
#, no-c-format
msgid "The &ktouch; main menu and program dialogs"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:336
#, no-c-format
msgid "In this chapter we look at some of the details in the program."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:337
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can use the <quote>What's this?</quote> feature (the little question mark "
"besides the minimize/maximize window buttons) in many dialogs of the program."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:343
#, no-c-format
msgid "The menu of &ktouch;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:344
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Pretty much everything in the &ktouch; menu should be self-explanatory. The "
"<guimenu>File</guimenu> menu contains options to load a user defined lecture "
"and edit lectures. The <guimenu>Training</guimenu> menu contains training "
"options and the <guimenu>settings</guimenu> menu contains options to setup the "
"program. The various options in the configuration dialog are explained in the "
"next section."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:353
#, no-c-format
msgid "Configuration options for &ktouch;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:354
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can customize &ktouch; quite a bit. The settings are always stored for the "
"current user of the program. Open the configuration dialog via <menuchoice>"
"<guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guimenuitem>Configure &ktouch; ...</guimenuitem>"
"</menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:360
#, no-c-format
msgid "General Options"
msgstr "Opcions generalas"

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:361
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this dialog you can change general options that control the behaviour of "
"&ktouch;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:365
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>General options</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:369
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>General options</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:372
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"First of all you can switch the sound on or off (might be useful in class "
"rooms). You can also set the sliding speed of the teacher's and student's line."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:376
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Most important, however, might be the selection of the lecture font. When "
"showing the teacher's text &ktouch; will normally use the font specified in the "
"lecture file. But if this font doesn't exist on your system or none has been "
"specified, &ktouch; uses a default system font. What if this font does not "
"contain all characters needed for your lecture? In such cases you can override "
"the choice of the font and specify any font you like."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:382
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"&ktouch; distinguishes between lecture and keyboard fonts. Overriding the "
"lecture font does not automatically adjust the keyboard font as well. Use the "
"appropriate option in the Keyboard Options configuration page."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:390
#, no-c-format
msgid "Training Options"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:391
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In this dialog you can select the options that control how &ktouch; changes "
"difficulty levels based on the user's skill."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:394
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Training options</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:398
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Training options</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:401
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If <guilabel>Automatic level adjustment</guilabel> is enabled, &ktouch; checks "
"the given limits at the end of each level and decides whether to increase or "
"decrease the level or stay in the level for another run. Level changes occur "
"always at the end of a level (&ie; when the user has finished the last line of "
"a level). If the typing speed (in characters per minute) and accuracy "
"(correctness) are <emphasis>both</emphasis> above or equal to the <guilabel>"
"Limits to increase a level</guilabel> &ktouch; will jump to the next higher "
"level. If <emphasis>either</emphasis> typing speed or accuracy falls below the "
"<guilabel>Limits to decrease a level</guilabel> &ktouch; will jump back to the "
"previous lower level."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:412
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"On this configuration page you can also select that the program remembers your "
"current level when you quit the program and jumps back to it when you restart "
"&ktouch;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:420
#, no-c-format
msgid "Keyboard Settings"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:421
#, no-c-format
msgid "In this dialog you can select keyboard specific options."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:423
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Keyboard settings</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:427
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Keyboard settings</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:430
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This dialog controls the appearance and functionality of the keyboard. Similar "
"to the <guilabel>Override lecture font</guilabel> option in the <guilabel>"
"General Options</guilabel> configuration page, you can here specify your own "
"keyboard font. The option <guilabel>Highlight keys on keyboard</guilabel> "
"can be used to decrease the difficulty of the training."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:435
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Once you are familiar with the keyboard you may want to turn keyboard "
"highlighting off so it won't distract you when you concentrate on the teacher's "
"text."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:443
#, no-c-format
msgid "Color Settings"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:444
#, no-c-format
msgid "In this dialog you can tweak the appearance of &ktouch;."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: screeninfo
#: index.docbook:446
#, no-c-format
msgid "<screeninfo>Color settings</screeninfo>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: index.docbook:450
#, no-c-format
msgid "<phrase>Color settings</phrase>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:453
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This configuration page does not need much explaination. You can choose among "
"four predefined color schemes for the keyboard. Alternatively you can switch "
"the color schemes <quote>on the fly</quote> using the menu option <menuchoice>"
"<guimenu>Settings</guimenu><guisubmenu>Color Schemes</guisubmenu></menuchoice>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:467
#, no-c-format
msgid "Extending &ktouch;"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:470
#, no-c-format
msgid "Creating your own lecture"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:471
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can easily create your own training lecture using the built-in &ktouch; "
"lecture editor. When you select <menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>"
"Edit Lecture...</guimenuitem></menuchoice> the <guilabel>"
"Open Lecture File</guilabel> dialog will open and ask you to select a lecture. "
"You can choose either edit the current lecture, any of the predefined lectures "
"(if you have write access to the installation directoy) or create an empty "
"lecture from scratch."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:480
#, no-c-format
msgid "Creating a lecture"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:481
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Let's assume you want to create a new lecture. There are a few guidelines you "
"may want to consider:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:488
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For the first 20 keys or so, only add 2 keys at a time to a new level. Let the "
"user practice the keys alone first in different combinations and then use the "
"new keys in words and mixed with already known words."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:494
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Add at least a few levels to the lecture in order to allow the user to see some "
"progress while training"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:497
#, no-c-format
msgid "Don't make the levels too long."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:501
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Try to increase the difficulty of the text gradually and test your text "
"yourself."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:506
#, no-c-format
msgid "After the first keys have been learned include a recap lecture."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:514
#, no-c-format
msgid "Things to know about lectures"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:515
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The lecture title is important for the quick-select menu entry in the training "
"menu and for the statistics. The comment should be used to include information "
"about the lecture author and source of lecture data if any."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:520
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"For each level you can specify the new characters/keys in the lecture. The text "
"you enter there will be shown as level information text in the top right info "
"box of the &ktouch; main window."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:525
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can also specify a default font for the lecture which will be used in the "
"teacher's and student's line and of course in the edit lecture dialog using the "
"<guibutton>Change Font...</guibutton> button."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: index.docbook:534
#, no-c-format
msgid "Creating your own keyboard"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:535
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You can create your own keyboards by creating keyboard files. Currently "
"&ktouch; doesn't have a keyboard editor but it is still pretty easy to define "
"your own keyboard. It doesn't require much more than a little math to work out "
"the geometry and some time. Best is to start with an existing keyboard file, "
"create a copy for your keyboard layout and simply adjust the lines."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:540
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Currently keyboard files are plain UTF-8 encoded text files. In one of the next "
"version of &ktouch; keyboard data will be stored in &XML; files, too. Therefore "
"I'll only briefly explain the structure of a keyboard file."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:545
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"A keyboard file usually starts with a header including some information about "
"the author and type of the keyboard."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:547
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"####################################################\n"
"#    &ktouch;\n"
"#    Keybord layout file for german keybord layout\n"
"#    Code=UTF-8\n"
"####################################################"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:548
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"All lines starting with a hash character '#' are considered comments. After the "
"header section a block of so called <quote>finger keys</quote> "
"follows, indicating the resting positions for all 8 fingers."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:553
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"#\n"
"#          Unicode  KeyChar  x    y\n"
"#\n"
"FingerKey  97       A        18   20\n"
"FingerKey  115      S        28   20\n"
"FingerKey  100      D        38   20\n"
"FingerKey  102      F        48   20\n"
"FingerKey  106      J        78   20\n"
"FingerKey  107      K        88   20\n"
"FingerKey  108      L        98   20\n"
"FingerKey  246      Ö        108  20"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:554
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Keys have the default dimensions of 8 units, so using a grid of 10 units gives "
"a normal looking keyboard. The first number is the unicode character code as "
"decimal number. The KeyText is the text printed on the key encoded as UTF-8 "
"(automatic under newer &Linux; versions, just make sure your editor saves files "
"as UTF-8 format)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:559
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The next section contains special keys, which are - apart from BackSpace and "
"Enter - just decorative."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:561
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"#\n"
"#           Unicode  KeyText    x     y     Width Height\n"
"#\n"
"ControlKey  260      Tab        0     10    15    10\n"
"ControlKey  13       Enter      138   20    12    10\n"
"ControlKey  258      Shift      123   30    27    10\n"
"ControlKey  264      AltGr      120   40    15    10\n"
"ControlKey  265      Ctrl       135   40    15    10\n"
"ControlKey  263      Alt        15    40    15    10\n"
"ControlKey  262      Strg       0     40    15    10\n"
"ControlKey  32       Leertaste  30    40    90    10\n"
"ControlKey  257      Shift      0     30    13    10\n"
"ControlKey  259      CapsLock   0     20    18    10\n"
"ControlKey  8        BackSpace  130   0     20    10"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:562
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The text you specify for the key is printed completely on the keyboard. "
"However, for the keytexts <quote>Shift</quote>, <quote>CapsLock</quote>, <quote>"
"Tab</quote>, <quote>BackSpace</quote> and <quote>Enter</quote> "
"&ktouch; draws the appropriate symbols. Hence you can use these keytexts "
"regardless of the language. The other keytexts like Ctrl or Alt need to be "
"translated though. The geometry of the control keys can be any rectangle, "
"defined by upper left x and y coordinates and width and height."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:570
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The next section contains all characters on the keyboard (except the already "
"defined finger keys) that can be typed <emphasis>without</emphasis> "
"pressing shift."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:573
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"#\n"
"#          Unicode  KeyChar   x    y      FingerKeyUnicode\n"
"#\n"
"NormalKey  94       ^         0    0      97\n"
"NormalKey  49       1         10   0      97\n"
"NormalKey  50       2         20   0      115\n"
"NormalKey  51       3         30   0      100\n"
"NormalKey  52       4         40   0      102\n"
"NormalKey  53       5         50   0      102\n"
"NormalKey  54       6         60   0      102\n"
".\n"
".\n"
".\n"
"NormalKey  46       .         103  30     108\n"
"NormalKey  45       -         113  30     246"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:574
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The definition of these primary keys or chars is pretty much the same as for "
"the finger keys, but they include an additional property. The last unicode "
"character code identifies the associated finger key. Basically which finger has "
"to be used to press this key."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:578
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The next and last section in a keyboard layout defines all keys that are "
"pressed by using a modifier key such as the shift key."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: index.docbook:582
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"#\n"
"#           Unicode   TargetUnicode   FingerUnicode   ControllUnicode    \n"
"Comment\n"
"#\n"
"HiddenKey   65        97              97              258                 #A\n"
"HiddenKey   66        98              102             258                 #B\n"
"HiddenKey   67        99              100             258                 #C\n"
"HiddenKey   68        100             100             258                 #D\n"
".\n"
".\n"
".\n"
"HiddenKey   124       60              97              264                 #|\n"
"HiddenKey   64        113             97              264                 #@"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:583
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <quote>hidden keys</quote> are really controlling what should happen on the "
"keyboard when you type a certain character. The first unicode number is the "
"character code of the character you define. The second number is the character "
"code of the key on the keyboard (one of the already defined normal keys). The "
"third number indicates a finger key (where does the finger rest that is needed "
"to type the key) and the last number indicates the modifier key you need to "
"press to get this character."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:591
#, no-c-format
msgid "Let's look at an example:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: index.docbook:592
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"You want to define the uppercase 'R' character. It has the unicode 82. The "
"character obtained when the 'R' key is pressed without modifier is the small "
"'r' with the unicode 114. The finger for the 'R' rests over the 'f' key which "
"has the unicode 102. And in order to get the 'R' you need to press the right "
"(or left) shift key which has the code 264 (this is actually not a unicode but "
"the code obtained from a keypress event of QT). Note that the identification of "
"controll keys will likely change in the next version of &ktouch;. But for now "
"you can use the codes used in other keyboard files."
msgstr ""

